          "act": 1,
          "act_title": "Stage 1: The Plessy v. Ferguson Precedent — How did the 1896 Plessy v. Ferguson ruling legally entrench racial segregation across the American South?",
          "title": "The Plessy v. Ferguson Precedent",
          "theme_heading": "The Plessy v. Ferguson Precedent",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In the early 1950s, seventeen southern states and Washington D.C. enforced compulsory racial separation by law, known as **de jure segregation**. This institutional system was anchored in the 1896 ruling of the **US Supreme Court** in *Plessy v. Ferguson*, which established the constitutional doctrine of \"separate but equal\". Southern state statutes mandated racial separation across public transport, classrooms, healthcare facilities, and recreational spaces.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Under the **Plessy v. Ferguson** precedent, southern municipal authorities insisted that racially segregated public accommodations fully satisfied the Fourteenth Amendment. In reality, public facilities designated for Black Americans were grossly inferior, dilapidated, and systematically starved of state tax funding. This legal fiction sustained an entrenched racial caste system that denied Black citizens basic constitutional dignity and equal protection across the American South.",
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In the early 1950s, seventeen southern states and Washington D.C. enforced compulsory racial separation by law, known as **de jure segregation**. This institutional system was anchored in the 1896 ruling of the **US Supreme Court** in *Plessy v. Ferguson*, which established the constitutional doctrine of \"separate but equal\". Southern state statutes mandated racial separation across public transport, classrooms, healthcare facilities, and recreational spaces.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Under the **Plessy v. Ferguson** precedent, southern municipal authorities insisted that racially segregated public accommodations fully satisfied the Fourteenth Amendment. In reality, public facilities designated for Black Americans were grossly inferior, dilapidated, and systematically starved of state tax funding. This legal fiction sustained an entrenched racial caste system that denied Black citizens basic constitutional dignity and equal protection across the American South."
          ],
