          "theme_heading": "The Brown v. Board Ruling",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In 1951, the NAACP consolidated five state legal challenges into the landmark class-action lawsuit **Brown v. Board of Education** of Topeka, Kansas. Oliver Brown challenged the school board after his daughter Linda was forced to travel across rail yards to a segregated Black school rather than attend a white school nearby. Chief counsel Thurgood Marshall argued that segregated public schooling violated the 14th Amendment's Equal Protection Clause.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Marshall supported his constitutional argument with psychological evidence, citing the famous **psychological doll tests** conducted by Kenneth and Mamie Clark, which demonstrated that state-enforced segregation generated deep feelings of racial inferiority among Black children. On 17 May 1954, **Chief Justice Earl Warren** delivered a unanimous 9–0 verdict, declaring that \"separate educational facilities are inherently unequal\", legally overturning the *Plessy* doctrine in public education.",
