          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> The *Brown* ruling provoked furious defiance across the white South, sparking a regional campaign of obstruction championed by Senator Harry Byrd as **Massive Resistance**. In 1956, over one hundred southern congressmen signed the **Southern Manifesto**, condemning the Supreme Court for judicial tyranny and pledging to resist school desegregation by all lawful means. White Citizens' Councils expanded rapidly, using economic boycotts and intimidation to deter Black applicants.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Southern opposition was further emboldened when the Supreme Court issued its 1955 implementation decree, known as **Brown II 'all deliberate speed'**. Because the Court failed to establish a firm compliance timetable, southern school boards exploited this ambiguous phrasing to delay integration indefinitely. By 1957, fewer than two percent of Black pupils in the former Confederacy attended racially integrated classrooms, rendering the original ruling practically unenforced.",
