          "theme": "Southern Massive Resistance",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> **Chief Justice Earl Warren & The Unanimous Verdict:** On 17 May 1954, Chief Justice Earl Warren delivered a unanimous 9–0 Supreme Court ruling. Warren forged total consensus to prevent segregationist dissent, declaring: *\"In the field of public education, the doctrine of 'separate but equal' has no place. Separate educational facilities are inherently unequal.\"* The 58-year-old legal precedent of Jim Crow was constitutionally broken.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> **The Flaw of Brown II & \"Massive Resistance\":** However, in May 1955 the Court issued *Brown II*, ordering desegregation to proceed only *\"with all deliberate speed\"*. Southern school boards exploited this vague phrase to delay integration indefinitely. Southern congressmen signed the \"Southern Manifesto\", pledging \"Massive Resistance\" and threatening to shut public schools rather than admit Black children."
