          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> **The \"Lost Year\" in Little Rock (1958–1959):** In September 1958, Governor Faubus escalated white resistance by shutting all four Little Rock public high schools for an entire year (the \"Lost Year\"), attempting to divert white pupils into segregated private academies. Only in 1959 did federal courts force Little Rock schools to reopen on an integrated basis.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> **The Reality of Desegregation by 1960:** The Little Rock crisis exposed the limits of judicial rulings without executive enforcement. By 1960, six years after *Brown v. Board*, fewer than 1% of Black children in the former Confederate states attended school with white children. The legal victory was won, but desegregation on the ground remained agonizingly slow."
