          "title": "The Arrest of Rosa Parks",
          "theme_heading": "The Arrest of Rosa Parks",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> On 1 December 1955 in Montgomery, Alabama, seasoned activist **Rosa Parks** boarded a city bus and sat in the 'colored' section. When the white rows filled, driver **James F. Blake** ordered Parks and three Black passengers to stand so a white man could sit. When Parks calmly refused to vacate her seat, Blake summoned police, who arrested and booked her.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> As secretary of the local NAACP, Parks possessed impeccable integrity that made her an ideal test plaintiff. Learning of her arrest, Jo Ann Robinson and the **Women's Political Council (WPC)** worked through the night, duplicating 35,000 leaflets calling on Montgomery's Black community to stage a one-day bus boycott on 5 December, the morning of Parks's trial.",
