          "title": "The 381-Day Carpool Operation",
          "theme_heading": "The 381-Day Carpool Operation",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> To sustain the boycott over months, the Montgomery Improvement Association established a highly sophisticated **300-car pool network**, operating forty-eight dispatch stations across the city. Black cab companies offered discounted fares matching bus tickets, while hundreds of volunteer drivers ferried workers and domestic servants to their jobs daily. Thousands of working-class men and women walked miles in all weather conditions, turning pedestrian travel into a badge of civic pride.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> The financial backbone of this logistical operation was the southern **Black church network**, which raised weekly relief funds and supplied venues for strategy meetings. The boycott exerted severe **economic attrition** on Montgomery's private transit company, which lost approximately sixty-five percent of its passenger revenue. White downtown merchants suffered devastating financial losses as Black shoppers boycotted segregated commercial districts, fracturing white business support for municipal segregation.",
