          "theme_heading": "Desegregated Buses Roll in Montgomery",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> On 20 December 1956, federal desegregation injunctions were formally served on Montgomery municipal officials, bringing the 381-day boycott to a triumphant conclusion. The following morning, Martin Luther King Jr., Ralph Abernathy, and E.D. Nixon boarded the city's first **integrated bus transit**, sitting in the front rows without incident. The boycott proved that disciplined collective economic boycotts could dismantle entrenched Jim Crow policies and win binding federal court protections.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> However, integration triggered furious **white backlash bombings** from white supremacists, who dynamited Black churches, bombed King's family home, and fired snipers at desegregated buses, temporarily halting night transit service. Despite violent intimidation, the Black community maintained strict discipline, establishing **non-violent direct action** as the defining moral strategy of the modern civil rights movement and elevating King to national prominence as its chief philosopher and spokesperson.",
