          "title": "The Murder of Emmett Till",
          "theme_heading": "The Murder of Emmett Till",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> In August 1955, fourteen-year-old Emmett Till visited relatives in Money, Mississippi. Accused of whistling at Carolyn Bryant, a white shopkeeper, Till was abducted from his great-uncle's home by **Roy Bryant & J.W. Milam**. The two white men brutally beat the youth, gouged out his eye, shot him, and dumped his body into the Tallahatchie River weighted with a gin fan.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> When Till's mutilated corpse was retrieved, his mother held a **Mamie Till-Mobley open casket** funeral in Chicago attended by fifty thousand mourners. She insisted the world see the horrific reality of southern racial terror. Photographs published in *Jet* magazine shocked the nation, making the **Emmett Till lynching** a defining catalyst that galvanized the young civil rights generation.",
