          "theme_heading": "The All-White Jury Acquittal",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> In September 1955, Roy Bryant and J.W. Milam stood trial for kidnapping and murder in Sumner, Mississippi. In an extraordinary act of southern courtroom courage, Emmett's sixty-four-year-old great-uncle, **Moses Wright courtroom testimony**, bravely rose on the witness stand, pointed his finger directly at the defendants, and publicly identified them as the armed men who abducted Emmett.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Despite compelling eyewitness testimony and medical evidence, the segregated panel delivered an **all-white jury acquittal** after just sixty-seven minutes. Four months later, protected from further prosecution by double jeopardy, Bryant and Milam sold their story to *Look* magazine for $4,000. Their brazen **Look magazine confession** bragging about the killing sparked intense moral outrage across northern states, exposing the utter corruption of Jim Crow justice.",
