          "title": "The Launch of the Freedom Rides",
          "theme_heading": "The Launch of the Freedom Rides",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> In May 1961, the Congress of Racial Equality launched the **CORE Freedom Rides** to test enforcement of the Supreme Court ruling in **Boynton v. Virginia** (1960), which outlawed racial segregation in interstate bus terminals. An initial group of thirteen Black and white activists boarded commercial buses in Washington D.C., intending to travel through Virginia, the Carolinas, Georgia, Alabama, and Mississippi to New Orleans.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> This courageous display of **interracial direct action** aimed to compel the federal government to enforce its own Supreme Court desegregation mandates. While upper-South states offered muted resistance, riders entered the Deep South anticipating violent confrontation, prepared to risk imprisonment and death to expose the ongoing lawlessness of southern segregationist authorities.",
