          "theme": "The Launch of the Freedom Rides",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> **Testing Federal Law: The CORE Freedom Rides:** On 4 May 1961, James Farmer and the Congress of Racial Equality (CORE) launched the \"Freedom Rides\". Interracial teams of Black and white activists boarded Greyhound and Trailways buses in Washington, D.C., bound for New Orleans, determined to test whether Southern authorities would obey the Supreme Court ruling banning segregation in interstate bus terminals.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> **The Anniston Firebomb & Birmingham Ambush:** On 14 May 1961, a Ku Klux Klan mob intercepted the Greyhound bus outside Anniston, Alabama, slashing its tires and firebombing the vehicle. In Birmingham, Police Commissioner \"Bull\" Connor allowed Klansmen fifteen minutes to beat the riders with iron pipes. Shocking international photographs of the burning bus forced the federal government to intervene."
