          "caption": "Plate 2.3: Police dog lunging at teenager Walter Gadsden during Project C in Birmingham, Alabama (3 May 1963), photographed by Bill Hudson.",
          "isReversed": false,
          "theme": "Project C & The Children's Crusade in Birmingham",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> **Project C in Birmingham (April 1963):** In April 1963, Dr Martin Luther King Jr., the SCLC, and Rev. Fred Shuttlesworth launched \"Project C\" (Confrontation) in Birmingham, Alabama—one of America's most segregated cities. Activists boycotted downtown stores and held sit-ins. When arrested, King penned his famous *Letter from Birmingham Jail*, declaring that \"justice too long delayed is justice denied.\"<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> **The Children's Crusade & Televised Outrage:** In May, thousands of schoolchildren joined the protests. Police Commissioner \"Bull\" Connor unleashed snarling police attack dogs and high-pressure fire hoses on the young marchers. Televised footage of children being knocked down by water jets horrified the world, forcing Birmingham business leaders to agree to desegregate downtown facilities."
