          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Despite the 1964 Act, southern Black citizens remained disenfranchised, with only two percent registered in **Dallas County voter registration** rolls in Selma, Alabama. In early 1965, the SCLC joined local activists in Selma to demand unobstructed voting rights. On 7 March 1965, six hundred peaceful marchers led by John Lewis set out toward the state capital of Montgomery.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> As marchers crossed the **Edmund Pettus Bridge**, Alabama state troopers and sheriff's posses attacked with tear gas, bullwhips, and nightsticks in the infamous **Bloody Sunday in Selma**. Televised footage of helpless marchers being clubbed unconscious provoked national outrage, leading King to issue an urgent national call for religious leaders and citizens to join the Selma voting campaign.",
