          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> **Enactment of the Voting Rights Act (6 Aug 1965):** Shocked by the televised brutality at Selma, President Lyndon B. Johnson addressed Congress, declaring that \"we shall overcome\". On 6 August 1965, Johnson signed the historic Voting Rights Act. The law banned all discriminatory literacy tests and empowered federal examiners to directly register Black voters in Southern counties where registration fell below 50%.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> **Transformative Impact & New Challenges:** The Voting Rights Act transformed Southern politics: within three years, Black voter registration in Mississippi jumped from 7% to over 60%. However, while federal legislation abolished *de jure* legal barriers in the South, it did not solve urban poverty, substandard ghetto housing, or police brutality in Northern cities."
