          "title": "The Mexico City Olympics Black Power Salute",
          "theme_heading": "The Mexico City Olympics Black Power Salute",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> The Black Power ethos reached an international television audience of hundreds of millions during the **1968 Mexico City Olympics**. African American sprinters **Tommie Smith & John Carlos** won gold and bronze medals in the 200 meters. Taking the medal podium barefoot to symbolize Black poverty, they wore civil rights badges and black gloves during the American national anthem.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> As \"The Star-Spangled Banner\" played, both athletes raised clenched fists with bowed heads in the famous **Olympic podium black fist salute**, protesting continuing racial oppression in America. The International Olympic Committee suspended both sprinters from the Olympic Village, but their defiant gesture became an enduring global icon of Black pride, sacrifice, and international resistance against entrenched racial inequality.",
