          "theme_heading": "The Watts Rebellion in Los Angeles",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In August 1965, only days after the Voting Rights Act was signed, explosive racial violence erupted in the Black neighborhood of Watts, Los Angeles. The **Watts riot Los Angeles (1965)** was ignited by a **Police brutality catalyst** during the aggressive traffic arrest of a young Black motorist, Marquette Frye, triggering six days of intense civil insurrection against entrenched racial discrimination.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Over fourteen thousand National Guardsmen were deployed to quell the uprising, which left **34 dead & $40m damage**, alongside over one thousand injuries and four thousand arrests. The rebellion stunned white Americans, exposing that northern and western Black communities suffered from chronic police harassment, dilapidated housing, and severe economic deprivation that southern civil rights laws could not fix.",
