          "theme_heading": "Dr King's Chicago Freedom Movement",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Recognizing that southern civil rights victories had not alleviated northern ghetto squalor, Dr King moved into a dilapidated West Side tenement in 1966. Leading the **Chicago Freedom Movement (1966)**, King organized mass non-violent protests against pervasive northern **Slum housing & redlining protest**, demanding open housing ordinances, fair employment practices, and an end to racist banking discrimination.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> When King led peaceful integration marches into all-white neighborhoods, he encountered a vicious **Marquette Park violent backlash**, where thousands of white residents threw bricks, bottles, and firecrackers, striking King in the head. King admitted he had never seen mobs as hostile as in Chicago, securing only vague promises from Mayor Richard Daley and discovering the intractable nature of northern de facto segregation.",
