          "isReversed": true,
          "theme": "The Draft System & Class Inequity",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> **A Discriminatory Draft System:** Opposition to the war centered on the military draft (Selective Service). Over two million young men were conscripted between 1965 and 1973. The draft system favoured the wealthy: middle-class youths obtained college deferments or doctor excuses, while working-class and minority men were sent to fight. Black Americans made up 11% of the US population but over 20% of combat deaths in the early war years.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> **Draft Resistance & Muhammad Ali:** In response, young men staged mass rallies to publicly burn draft cards, while thousands fled to Canada. In 1967, world boxing champion Muhammad Ali refused the draft on moral and religious grounds, declaring: \"I ain't got no quarrel with them Vietcong.\" The draft galvanized millions of students into active protest."
