          "theme": "Exposure of the My Lai Massacre",
          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> **The Slaughter at My Lai (16 March 1968):** On 16 March 1968, American troops of Charlie Company, commanded by Lieutenant William Calley, entered the village of My Lai. Frustrated by booby-trap casualties and finding no enemy soldiers, the unit murdered up to 504 unarmed Vietnamese civilians—mostly women, children, and elderly men—herding them into ditches and executing them.<br><br><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> **Exposure & Shattered Moral Authority:** The massacre was concealed by military officials until November 1969, when investigative reporter Seymour Hersh published the story alongside horrifying photographs of bodies piled in ditches. The revelations destroyed the belief that the US was fighting a noble war, severely deepening anti-war feeling across America."
