          "title": "Congressional Hawks & The Fear of Humiliation",
          "theme_heading": "Congressional Hawks & The Fear of Humiliation",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> In the halls of Congress, powerful conservative **congressional hawks** from southern and midwestern states vehemently defended the military mission. Conservative lawmakers argued that premature American withdrawal would embolden Soviet and Chinese aggression worldwide, betray thousands of fallen soldiers, and inevitably lead to the domino-effect fall of Thailand, Malaysia, and the Philippines to hostile communist insurgencies.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Above all, political leaders were gripped by the profound **fear of national humiliation**, refusing to preside over the first military defeat in United States history. This hawkish political stance aligned with a widespread **patriotic working-class backlash**, where blue-collar voters resented privileged college students who avoided military service while their own sons were drafted and killed in Vietnamese jungles.",
