          "pathway": "Starter Hook",
          "teacher_guidance": "Analyzes President Richard Nixon's landmark 3 November 1969 televised address appealing to the \"great silent majority\" of non-protesting, patriotic working- and middle-class Americans. Recommended Watch Window: 00:00–01:34 • Lesson Phase: Quick Starter Check (0–3m). Tone Alert: Presidential speech analysis and social commentary.",
          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[00:31] Quick Check:</strong> Who did President Nixon define as the \"great silent majority\" in his historic 3 November 1969 national television address?</li><li><strong>[01:02] Hinge Question:</strong> Why did Nixon warn that an immediate, unilateral US withdrawal from Vietnam would lead to a catastrophic \"collapse of confidence in American leadership\" across the world?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Quick Check:</strong> The vast millions of ordinary, law-abiding working- and middle-class Americans who did not participate in anti-war street demonstrations, countercultural protests, or media debates.</li><li><strong>Hinge Question:</strong> Nixon framed the issue not around the merits of Vietnam itself, but around global credibility. He argued that if the world's greatest superpower surrendered under domestic mob pressure, allies in Europe and Asia would conclude that American security guarantees were worthless, while the Soviet Union and China would be emboldened to launch aggressive communist expansion globally.</li></ol>",
          "id": "Hqz0i83Jqxg"
