          "act": 5,
          "act_title": "Stage 5: The Collapse of the American Domestic Home Front — How did the loss of American domestic political will ultimately decide the outcome of the Vietnam War?",
          "title": "The Collapse of the American Domestic Home Front",
          "theme_heading": "The Collapse of the American Domestic Home Front",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Ultimately, the Vietnam War was lost on the American home front through the profound **collapse of domestic consensus**. As the conflict dragged on with no end in sight, the mounting human toll—totaling **58,220 American military deaths** and over 300,000 wounded—shattered the post-WWII public belief in American military invincibility, moral righteousness, and unquestioned global policing authority across the globe.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> The catastrophic defeat produced the enduring **'Vietnam Syndrome'**, a deep national reluctance to commit American ground forces to protracted foreign interventions without clear exit strategies and overwhelming public support. The war severely divided American society, destroyed trust in presidential integrity, and permanently altered the relationship between the American people, the press, and the federal government.",
