            "model": "<p>Interpretation 2 presents a compelling argument that President Nixon's resignation in August 1974 was the direct, inevitable result of the 'Smoking Gun' tape and the consequent collapse of his congressional support. Nixon had consistently claimed executive privilege to conceal White House recordings. However, on 24 July 1974, the Supreme Court ruled unanimously in *United States v. Nixon* that presidential privilege did not shield criminal evidence. The subpoenaed tape from 23 June 1972 proved that Nixon had ordered Chief of Staff H.R. Haldeman to use the CIA to halt the FBI's investigation. On 7 August 1974, senior Republican leaders informed Nixon he faced certain conviction in the Senate. Therefore, Interpretation 2 is supported because the definitive legal proof of obstruction destroyed his political survival.</p><p>However, Interpretation 1 offers a vital counter-perspective by arguing that the crisis was engineered over two years of relentless investigative journalism and congressional scrutiny. Reporters Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein at the *Washington Post* uncovered secret CREEP campaign slush funds, while the televised Senate Watergate Committee chaired by Sam Ervin gripped 85% of American households. Furthermore, Alexander Butterfield's sensational disclosure of the White House taping system provided the key that allowed Special Prosecutor Archibald Cox to demand evidence, despite Nixon's 'Saturday Night Massacre'. Therefore, Interpretation 1 is valid because sustained democratic oversight prevented the initial burglary from being successfully buried.</p><p>In conclusion, I agree with Interpretation 2 to a large extent. While congressional investigations and journalism (Interpretation 1) uncovered the sprawling conspiracy, it was the unassailable audio proof of the 'Smoking Gun' tape (Interpretation 2) that stripped Nixon of his remaining party defenders and forced his resignation on 9 August 1974.</p>",
